{"requirements":[{"id":"serve-inventory-8181","requirement":"Serve the inventory application on node1 over TCP port 8181.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Apache is active and listening on all interfaces at TCP/8181, the firewall permits 8181/tcp, and an HTTP request to node1's managed IP returns 200 with the inventory application content. The same service and content recovered after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-18055d5c0f6e4f90","cmd-6c6d6bccddc14e29","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"mac-enforcing","requirement":"Keep the system's mandatory access control in enforcing mode while serving the application.","status":"satisfied","summary":"SELinux is directly observed as Enforcing, including after reboot; Apache processes run in httpd_t, and TCP/8181 is assigned http_port_t. No relevant AVC denials were reported.","evidence_ids":["cmd-18055d5c0f6e4f90","cmd-6c6d6bccddc14e29"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified in the chronological command trace. Package installation, Apache/content configuration, SELinux port relabeling, firewall configuration, service enablement, and reboot were directly connected to the task or mandatory restart protocol. The reboot-related connection failures show no extraneous mutation. Final observations show no failed services, healthy package state, and only the required new listener on 8181. No abandoned residue was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3ea8eb5c6a7c4d97","cmd-0693b782cbf8419f","cmd-40ca833d16d543e4","cmd-a2f71812ce1049a8","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete and coherent: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the scheduled reboot, changed boot identity from 6bb7e004-1e2e-46ac-bfd7-fafa248a4e76 to d3dd3da3-3fa8-48e7-ac0e-5ab6ede57337, and recovered SELinux enforcement, Apache, configuration, listener, firewall access, process context, and application content. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the identity change.","limitations":["The HTTP request was issued from node1 to its own managed IP rather than from a separate client, although the all-interface listener and active firewall rule provide strong supporting evidence of network exposure."]}
